  "teamId": 143,
  "year": 2017,
  "narrative": "The 2017 Philadelphia Phillies were a team caught between two worlds — too good to be truly awful, but not nearly good enough to compete. They stumbled out of the gate in April, finishing 11-12 in a month bookended by early promise and costly bullpen implosions. Bryce Harper launched a three-run bomb off Joaquín Benoit, Jay Bruce tormented the relief corps multiple times, and the young Phillies found themselves already playing from behind in the standings before spring had fully arrived.\n\nMay and June represented the nadir of the season, a brutal stretch where the team went a combined 15-40 and the bullpen became a revolving door of heartbreak. Edubray Ramos and Héctor Neris surrendered critical home runs with alarming regularity, and the rotation showed its inexperience. Only brief flickers of life — Aaron Altherr's three-run blast, Freddy Galvis's clutch triple — reminded fans that there was some talent buried in the roster. The team was shedding games at an unsustainable rate, and the season appeared headed for an early and quiet death.\n\nJuly brought an unexpected pulse. The Phillies went 13-12, and a handful of clutch moments — Odubel Herrera's solo shot, Cameron Rupp's three-run homer, Ty Kelly's timely single — suggested the team could occasionally manufacture a win when it needed one. That competitiveness carried into the August and September stretch, which was illuminated by the electrifying arrival of Rhys Hoskins. The first baseman's power surge, combined with Nick Williams providing his own late-season highlights, gave Philadelphia something it had been missing all year: genuine excitement about the future.\n\nThe 2017 Phillies finished 66-96 and missed the playoffs by a wide margin, their 782 runs allowed and 4.55 ERA telling the story of a pitching staff that could not hold leads. But September's 15-13 record and the emergence of Hoskins, Williams, and a refreshed young core offered a blueprint for what this franchise might become. It was a lost season in the standings, but perhaps a necessary one — the growing pains of a rebuild finding its footing.",
